  "query_preview": "..Utah and southwest Wyoming remain under the\ninfluence of high pressure this morning, with the ridge axis\ncentered roughly over Utah. This is bringing mostly clear skies\nwith generally light flow aloft. Lingering monsoon moisture\nremains in place, with satellite derived PWs primarily in the 0.7\nto 0.9 inch range, in the 75th to 90th percentile for this time of\nyear. This should be more than enough moisture for convective\ninitiation over the higher terrain this afternoon, but the lack of\nflow and lack of additional instability will result in a more\nlimited valley threat than the past few days. Highs today will\naverage about 5F above seasonal normals.\n\nLittle change in the weather pattern is anticipated through\nThursday, with the ridge axis creeping ever so slightly east each\nday, eventually returning to the Four Corners by Friday. Thus,\nconvective threat will remain mostly unchanged with a subtle\nwarming trend. By Wednesday into Thursday, portions of far \nsouthern Utah will see isolated pockets of HeatRisk values in the \nred (major) range, but at this time the elevated risk of heat\nrelated illness does not look widespread enough to warrant heat\nrelated headlines. Additionally, will see enough drying during\nthis time for RH values in western and southern valley locations\nto approach critical levels, though winds will remain generally\nlight.\n\nRather than parking near the Four Corners for the weekend,\nguidance is increasingly suggesting that the ridge will continue\neast, potentially opening up the West to a more active pattern for\nthe weekend and into early next week. The ridge should initially\nlimit the influence of a Pacific Northwest storm system over the\nweekend, keeping it as primarily a grazing feature for northern\nportions of the area. Main impact will be on temperatures as the\nassociated front moves through Saturday, with maxes up to 5F below\nseasonal normals anticipated by early next week. The resultant\nincrease in instability will also bring the potential for more\nwidespread convective activity, particularly for northern Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming. With the ridge then most likely out of the\npicture, at least for the time, troughs will have the potential to\nbring more significant impacts for the forecast area. However, any\ndetails on the potential timing, track, and strength of any of\nthese systems remains low at this time.",
